proof_profiler_memory (the experimental per-command memory profiler) — implementation plan
============================================================================================

status: implemented, build/AFP demo pending. spec:
D-2026-08-13-proof-profiler-memory. one of three independent profiler
experiments landing in parallel (time, memory, proof-size-delta) — each
its own theory, its own tool, its own PR; no shared state between them
beyond the pattern they all borrow from ir/ir.ML's exec_text.

advertised metadata
--------------------

  name          profile_proof_memory
  description   see the `mcp_tool` declaration in
                mcp/Tools/HOL/MCP_Profile_Memory.thy — the description
                text there IS the advertised description, kept in one
                place rather than duplicated here.
  inputSchema   theory_name :: string, isar_text :: source
  annotations   read_only

why `theory_name` / `isar_text`, not `theory` / `text`: both of the
obvious names are themselves Isar outer-syntax command keywords, and a
command keyword used as a param NAME truncates the `mcp_tool`
declaration's own span at the outer-syntax scanning pass, before the
params-clause parser ever runs — the same class of hazard
`MCP_Tools.thy`'s `ptyp_parser` documents for `term`/`typ` as TYPE
names, but that comment doesn't cover param names and this cost real
debugging time to rediscover. Renamed to match `MCP_Repl.thy`'s own
dispatcher argument-key conventions.

backing mechanism
------------------

Full narrative (the Poly/ML API archaeology, the two dead-end signals,
why a forced-GC live-byte reading is the one that works) lives in the
spec section itself (D-2026-08-13-proof-profiler-memory) and in
MCP_Profile_Memory.thy's own theory-level `text` block — not repeated
here. Summary:

- input resolves `theory_name` via `Thy_Info.get_theory` (same lookup
  `repl_init`'s `theories` and `proof_tasks` use) and builds a FRESH,
  throwaway `Toplevel.state` exactly the way `Ir.init`'s `from_specs`
  branch does for a single theory spec (`Theory.begin_theory` +
  `Toplevel.make_state`, ir/ir.ML:439-462). This tool never touches
  `Ir.repl_tab` — it cannot mutate a live `repl_step` repl, and no
  `Ir` function is called at all. Not a modification of the
  verbatim-reused ir.ML; new ML alongside it.
- `isar_text` is parsed once via `Outer_Syntax.parse_text`. The
  "<ignored>" pseudo-transitions the parser interleaves for
  whitespace/comment gaps (a no-op `Keep`, confirmed via
  `isabelle console`, not guessed) are dropped before profiling so a
  report line always corresponds to a real command.
- each remaining transition runs through `Toplevel.command_exception`
  in turn. Immediately before and after, `ML_Heap.full_gc ()` forces a
  collection and `ML_Statistics.get ()`'s `size_heap -
  size_heap_free_last_full_GC` fields are read and diffed — this pair
  is reachable post-bootstrap even though `PolyML.Statistics` itself
  is hidden from ordinary theories (Pure/ML_Bootstrap.thy swaps in a
  stub `PolyML` for everything built after Pure; `ML_Statistics`/
  `ML_Heap` survive as already-compiled closures over the pre-swap
  structure). The reported number is therefore NET bytes retained by
  the command, not gross allocation — a command that allocates heavily
  but reclaims nearly all of it before the next sample reads as cheap.
  Deliberate: retained state is what compounds across a proof script;
  reclaimed churn does not. Cost: two forced full GCs per profiled
  command — slow, accepted for an experimental tool that wants a real
  number over a fast, meaningless one (the two signals that don't need
  a forced GC — a raw `size_allocation - size_allocation_free` delta,
  or a raw `size_heap` delta — were tried first and are both useless
  once a session's heap has grown past its first few theories; see the
  spec section for the measured numbers).
- error handling: PARTIAL RESULTS, not whole-call failure. Profiling
  stops at the first failing command (later commands may depend on its
  state) and that row is reported ABORTED with the error message;
  every command before it still carries a full reading.
- output: one line per command (index, source line, keyword, bytes
  retained or ABORTED+message), sorted highest-first, plus a summary
  line (commands profiled, total retained, where it stopped if it
  did).

