ASSUMPTIONS -> tests
----------------------

- A1 `ML_Statistics`/`ML_Heap` are reachable from an ordinary theory's
  ML block even though `PolyML.Statistics` is not. CONFIRMED
  (mechanism section above); the theory would fail to compile
  otherwise, so every build IS this test.
- A2 a forced-GC live-byte reading (`size_heap -
  size_heap_free_last_full_GC` immediately after `ML_Heap.full_gc`)
  moves measurably and correctly-signed for a known large allocation,
  where the two unforced signals do not. [ml unit: "large allocation
  ranks above trivial proof" section, MCP_Profile_Memory_Tests.thy]
- A3 the statistics interface is PROCESS-WIDE: concurrent activity
  from unrelated requests can pollute a reading, and forcing a GC does
  not isolate this call from other threads' allocations racing with
  it. NOT mitigated — recorded as a known limitation, not tested (no
  reasonable single-process test demonstrates an absence of
  interference; documented instead in the spec section and the tool's
  own description text).
- A4 profiling never creates or touches an `Ir.repl_tab` entry. [ml
  unit: "isolation" section — repls listing unchanged before/after a
  profile call, modulo unrelated concurrent test churn]
- A5 error handling is partial-results-then-stop, not whole-call
  failure. [ml unit: "error handling" section]
